              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"\n\nThis famous quote is attributed to the 14th-century Arab polymath, sociologist, and historian **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406). It is found in his 1377 magnum opus, the ***Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena* or *Introduction*). \n\n### The Context of the Quote\nIn the *Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun explores the rise and fall of civilizations, presenting early theories on sociology, demography, and economics\u2014prefiguring many concepts later popularized by Adam Smith and classical economists. \n\nThe quote arises from his analysis of **state-run commerce** and the dangers of rulers or governments trying to engage in trade and business. \n\nIbn Khaldun argued that:\n1. **The Danger of State Monopolies:** When a ruler or government tries to engage in business, they inherently use their coercive political power to buy goods at artificially low prices and sell them at artificially high prices. This destroys fair competition and chokes out private merchants.\n2. **The Erosion of Incentives:** When private enterprise is forced to compete with the state, the incentive to produce, innovate, and trade diminishes, which ultimately ruins the tax base and weakens the entire state or dynasty. \n3. **The Power of Discipline:** Real, sustainable economic growth comes from the \"responsible and organized merchant.\" These are individuals who must manage their risks, watch their capital, understand market forces, and remain disciplined because they do not have the safety net of tax revenues or military power. \n\nThus, Ibn Khaldun asserts that businesses managed with market discipline, diligence, and organizational responsibility will always prove more resilient, efficient, and ultimately successful in the long run than state-owned enterprises or those operated by powerful rulers relying on sheer political dominance.",
